WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.020
Block the germ routes: clean hands and a routine that works.

2
00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:11.490
This is the video for module 1 of Home Hygiene and Sanitation: Germs, Clean Hands and a Cleaning Routine.

3
00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:20.850
Most stomach illnesses in our homes, like diarrhoea, cholera and typhoid, are caused by germs that live in human faeces.

4
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:28.570
A germ is a living thing too small to see. So a clean-looking cup or clear water can still carry germs.

5
00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:36.750
WHO notes that most people infected with cholera have no symptoms, yet they can pass the bacteria in their faeces.

6
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:44.910
Germs make you ill only when they reach the mouth. Every hygiene habit in this course simply blocks that route.

7
00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:57.770
Health workers use the F-diagram. It shows five routes, all starting with F. The strongest barrier is a clean toilet that keeps faeces in, so germs never escape at all.

8
00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:04.500
Fluids. Faeces leak into a well, or a dirty cup is dipped into stored drinking water.

9
00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:11.980
Fingers. Hands touch faeces when using the toilet or cleaning a child's bottom, then touch food.

10
00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:16.900
Flies land on open faeces, then on uncovered food.

11
00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:23.760
Fields: children play where someone squatted. And food carries germs from all the other routes.

12
00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:35.850
For example, Bisi lives in a compound in Ibadan where four households share one tap and one toilet. On Thursday morning, three children have watery diarrhoea.

13
00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:46.420
Germs have a waiting period before symptoms. For cholera, it is 12 hours to 5 days. So she looks back days, not just to the last meal.

14
00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:57.650
She walks the routes. One household dips cups into an open water bucket. The toilet door does not close, and flies gather. There is no soap near the toilet.

15
00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:06.940
She does not try to prove the exact cause. She gets a lidded water container with a tap, soap beside the toilet, and a repaired door.

16
00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:19.520
And because watery diarrhoea can be cholera, the children start oral rehydration solution, called ORS, and go to the health centre. Severe cholera can kill within hours.

17
00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:30.950
Water alone is not enough. Soap lifts grease and dirt, and rubbing and rinsing carry germs away. Wet your hands and use enough soap to cover every surface.

18
00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:36.060
Rub palm to palm, then each palm over the back of the other hand.

19
00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:41.960
Rub with fingers interlaced, and the backs of your fingers against the opposite palm.

20
00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:52.060
Rub each thumb, then the fingertips in a circle in the other palm. Most people miss these, and they are exactly what touches food and faces.

21
00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:58.710
Rinse, dry with a single-use towel, not your wrapper, and use the towel to turn off the tap.

22
00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:06.230
Most people stop after about 10 seconds. Count slowly to 40, or sing a short song twice.

23
00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:14.610
For example, Halima in Kano makes a hole near the bottom of a 5-litre jerrycan and hangs soap in an onion net.

24
00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:21.980
She counts 20 washes from one full jerrycan, so each wash uses 0.25 litres.

25
00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:27.670
Six people washing at 5 key moments a day. That is 30 washes.

26
00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:34.340
So she needs 7.5 litres a day, and refills in the morning and again after lunch.

27
00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:42.780
One bar lasts 10 days, so she buys soap in a pack of three. She places the station between the toilet and the house.

28
00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:47.500
Cleaning removes dirt and many germs with soap and scrubbing.

29
00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:51.140
Dirt protects germs, so cleaning always comes first.

30
00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:53.960
Most of the house only needs good cleaning.

31
00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:59.070
Work top to bottom, and cleanest to dirtiest, with a separate cloth for the toilet.

32
00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:04.420
Disinfect where faeces, vomit or raw meat juices may have been.

33
00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:14.560
Follow the label dilution, wear gloves, open the window, and never mix bleach with toilet acids or ammonia, because it can release dangerous gas.

34
00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:16.810
To remember.

35
00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:18.660
Block all five routes.

36
00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:20.930
Keep faeces in a safe toilet.

37
00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:23.640
Wash hands the WHO way at the key moments.

38
00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:25.760
Clean before you disinfect.

39
00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:33.520
Wipe door handles, switches and taps every day, and share the weekly work fairly, with bleach tasks kept for adults.

40
00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:46.050
Now map your home on the F-diagram and fix one gap, build and test your handwashing station, and post a cleaning rota where everyone can see it. Continue with the lessons below.
